## `ledgerkit.loader`

### `load_journal(path: str | os.PathLike) -> Journal`

The main multi-file entry point. Resolves `path`, recursively expands
every `include <path>` directive it finds by **textual substitution**
— not an AST-level merge — producing one combined text string that is
parsed exactly once via `parse_string()`
(loader@v1#loader-005). As a direct consequence, directive scope
active before an `include` line (for example an `alias` directive)
propagates naturally into the included content, because the included
text is spliced in at the exact textual position of the `include` line
within one continuous parse (loader@v1#loader-005).

`include` detection requires the literal keyword `include` at column 0
followed by one or more whitespace characters; an indented `include`
line is never recognised as a directive at all
(loader@v1#loader-001). The include target is resolved via fixed
precedence: a `~`-prefixed path is expanded against the user's home
directory; an absolute path is used as-is; otherwise the path is
resolved relative to the *containing file's own directory* (the
immediate parent file for a nested include, not the process's current
working directory and not necessarily the root file's directory)
(loader@v1#loader-002). If the resolved path string contains a glob
metacharacter (`*`, `?`, `[`), it is expanded via a recursive glob and
the matches sorted; a target that collides with its own containing
file is always filtered out of the result before further processing
(loader@v1#loader-002, loader@v1#loader-003).

Circular includes are detected via an ancestor-stack set threaded
through the recursive expansion (a path is tracked only while it is
currently "in progress" along the active include chain, not globally
across every file ever included) — a target already on that stack
raises `ParseError("circular include detected: ...")`
(loader@v1#loader-004). Because the stack models ancestry rather than
global history, a "diamond" include graph (two sibling files that both
independently include the same third file) does **not** trigger
circular-include detection: the shared file's content is expanded and
merged twice, with no warning, and `Journal.included_files` reports
only the count of distinct files touched, not the number of times
content was duplicated (loader@v1#loader-004).

A `ParseError` raised while parsing the expanded text is re-attributed
back to its true originating file and local line number (not the line
number in the flattened blob) via an internal line-to-source map built
during expansion; this remap only fires when the error's line number
falls within the map's range, otherwise the original, untranslated
exception propagates unchanged (loader@v1#loader-008).

Missing-file handling branches on whether the raw include target
string was itself glob-shaped: if so and no files match, `ParseError`
("no files matched") is raised; if not, and the single resolved target
does not exist, `FileNotFoundError` is raised instead, naming the
resolved absolute path (loader@v1#loader-007). Unsupported file
extensions (anything other than `.journal`/`.ledger`) and recognised
hledger format-type prefixes (e.g. `csv:`, `timedot:`) on an include
target are always rejected with `ParseError`, checked before the
not-found logic applies (loader@v1#loader-007). This two-outcome
dichotomy for a missing target is **not fully exhaustive**: when a
containing directory's own name happens to contain a glob
metacharacter, a plain, non-glob include target resolved inside it can
be silently reclassified as a glob internally, match zero files, and
raise an uncaught `IndexError` instead of either documented exception
— a confirmed, reproducible gap rather than a hypothetical one
(loader@v1#loader-002, loader@v1#loader-003,
loader@v1#loader-007). Separately, a file that includes itself by its
own literal filename is filtered out by the self-reference guard and
then reported as `FileNotFoundError` even though the file demonstrably
exists on disk — a misleading, but currently real, error message
(loader@v1#loader-003).

### `load_journal_stdin() -> Journal`

Reads all of standard input and parses it directly; it does **not**
perform include expansion at all.

### `merge_journals(journals: list[Journal]) -> Journal`

A separate public function from the include mechanism. Merges
already-parsed `Journal` objects by order-preserving concatenation of
`transactions`, `prices`, `declared_accounts`, `declared_commodities`,
`declared_payees`, and `declared_tags` across all inputs
(loader@v1#loader-006). `source_file` on the result is taken from the
first input only; `included_files` is the arithmetic sum of each
input's own `included_files`. A single-element input list is a fast
path returning the original object unchanged (`is` identity, not a
copy); an empty input list raises `ValueError`
(loader@v1#loader-006). Because `merge_journals` operates on already
-parsed objects rather than splicing source text, directive scope
captured within one journal's own parse cannot propagate into another
journal being merged this way — the two merge mechanisms are not
interchangeable (loader@v1#loader-005).

