## `ledgerkit.tags`

### `parse_tags(comment: Optional[str]) -> list[tuple[str, str]]`

Parses hledger-style `name:value` tags out of a comment string (an
empty or `None` comment yields `[]`). The grammar implemented is more
permissive, and in one respect stricter, than hledger's own manual
prose suggests:

- A tag name has no character-class restriction beyond "not a colon,
  no internal whitespace, and the candidate text must not end in
  whitespace" — digits, punctuation, and non-ASCII characters are all
  accepted (tags@v1#tags-007).
- A space immediately before a colon voids that entire tag candidate —
  not merely producing an empty-string name — and scanning resumes
  right after the voided colon, so a genuine tag appearing later on
  the same line is still found (tags@v1#tags-005).
- A tag's value runs from just after the colon to the next literal
  comma or end of line; a colon *inside* a value does not terminate
  it. There is no escaping mechanism for a comma inside a value, so a
  value can never contain a literal comma — this is treated as a
  genuine hledger grammar limitation, not a ledgerkit omission
  (tags@v1#tags-006).
- The same tag name may legitimately repeat, with different values, in
  one result; `parse_tags` always returns an ordered list of pairs,
  never a dict collapsed by name (tags@v1#tags-007).

### `effective_date(txn, posting) -> date`

Returns the posting's own `date_override` if set, otherwise the
transaction's `date` (tags@v1#tags-008).

### `effective_date2(txn, posting) -> date`

A four-level fallback chain, each level checked in order, stopping at
the first value that is not `None`: the posting's own `date2_override`;
then the transaction's `date2`; then the posting's own
`date_override` (its *primary* override, reused here as a
secondary-date fallback); then the transaction's primary `date`
(tags@v1#tags-008).

### Tag inheritance and effective-tags computation

Beyond `parse_tags` itself, `tags.py` implements the inheritance and
union logic that other parts of ledgerkit (notably query evaluation,
see `ledgerkit.query` below) rely on for tag matching, even though the
specific helper functions that do this work are private
(`_inherited_account_tags`, `_effective_tags`,
`_accounts_effective_tags`, `_posting_commodities`,
`_commodity_tags`). Documenting their behaviour here because public
query matching depends on it:

- An account inherits declared tags from itself and every strict
  ancestor in its `:`-separated hierarchy (concatenated
  least-specific-first); a sibling branch does not inherit a
  different sibling's tags at any depth (tags@v1#tags-004).
- The full "effective tags" of a (transaction, posting) pair is the
  union of four independent sources — the posting's own tags, the
  transaction's own tags, the posting's account's inherited tags, and
  the posting's commodity's declared tags — concatenated with **no
  shadowing, precedence, or deduplication of any kind**, including
  when two sources declare the exact same `(name, value)` pair
  (tags@v1#tags-001, tags@v1#tags-002). This is an explicit,
  documented design choice: hledger's own manual prose describes an
  override order, but ledgerkit's implementation deliberately does not
  replicate that as precedence.
- A second, narrower view used for `accounts`-style tag matching
  unions only the transaction's own tags and the account's inherited
  tags, explicitly excluding the posting's own tags and any
  commodity-propagated tags (tags@v1#tags-003). This narrower view's
  actual wiring to a specific caller (stated in its own docstring to
  be `ledgerkit.reports.accounts`, via `ledgerkit.query`'s
  accounts-mode dispatch) was not independently verified against that
  caller within the permitted evidence for this topic — only the
  function's own, directly-tested behaviour in isolation is confirmed
  (tags@v1#tags-003).
- Because `Posting.amount` is a single `Amount`, not a collection, the
  per-posting commodity list consumed by the commodity-tag lookup is
  always zero or one element in the current data model, even though
  the underlying helper is shaped to accept an arbitrary-length list
  for forward compatibility (tags@v1#tags-009).

