## `ledgerkit.commodity_style`

### `CommodityStyle`

A dataclass with fields `commodity: str`, `prefix: bool = True`,
`space: bool = False`, `decimal_mark: str = "."`, `group_separator: str
= ""`, `precision: int = 2`.

#### `CommodityStyle.infer(commodity: str, raw_amount_str: str) -> CommodityStyle` (classmethod)

Determines prefix-vs-suffix placement by plain string matching: it
strips a leading minus sign, then checks whether the remainder starts
with the commodity symbol (prefix) or ends with it (suffix); if a
symbol degenerately matches both ends, the prefix branch wins because
it is checked first (commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-001). If
the symbol matches neither end — a case the implementation's own
comment calls a rare edge case — `prefix` stays `True` and the
*entire* stripped string, including any unrelated text, is treated as
the numeric portion fed into decimal/group-separator inference; this
is not a benign no-op, since it can silently corrupt the inferred
`precision` by counting non-digit characters toward the digit count
(commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-001).

Decimal-mark and group-separator inference, given the numeric
substring once the commodity symbol is stripped:

- If both `.` and `,` are present, whichever occurs rightmost in the
  string is the decimal mark and the other is the group separator,
  regardless of how many times either character repeats
  (commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-003).
- If only one of the two separator characters is present, and it
  occurs exactly once, is not at position 0, and is followed by
  exactly three digits, it is classified as a digit-group separator
  (precision becomes 0); in every other single-separator case it is
  the decimal mark, with precision equal to the digit count after its
  last occurrence (commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-002).

#### `CommodityStyle.format(self, quantity: Decimal) -> str`

Formats a `Decimal` according to the style's fields. Negative-sign
placement is asymmetric by design, matching hledger's own convention:
for a prefix-style commodity the sign goes immediately after the
symbol and before the number, with the normal `space` gap dropped
entirely in the negative case (e.g. `"£-5,000.00"`, even when the
positive form would be `"£ 5,000.00"`); for a suffix-style commodity
the sign goes before the number (ahead of its normal leading position)
and the `space` gap before the trailing symbol is preserved as usual
(e.g. `"-1,234.56 EUR"`) (commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-004).

#### `CommodityStyle.parse_style_override(style_string: str) -> CommodityStyle` (classmethod)

Backs the CLI's `-c`/`--commodity-style` flag, but is equally usable
as a Python API for constructing a `CommodityStyle` from a sample
string. It extracts the commodity symbol by trying a suffix-alphabetic
match first (e.g. picking `"USD"` out of `"1,000.00 USD"`), and only on
failure tries a leading-symbol match (e.g. `"$"` out of `"$1,000.00"`).
It raises `ValueError` if the string is empty, contains no digit
characters at all, or contains digits but matches neither pattern (for
example a bare numeric string with no commodity symbol)
(commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-006). An override constructed
this way only affects the formatting of the one commodity symbol it
names — it never changes any other commodity's inferred style, even
within the same report run (commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-006).
The exact CLI-side plumbing that merges a successfully-parsed override
into a report's per-commodity styles map lives in `ledgerkit.cli`, and
was not independently inspected as part of this topic's evidence
(commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-006).

