## `ledgerkit.query`

The public surface re-exported from `ledgerkit/query/__init__.py`
funnels two distinct inputs — a hand-written query string, and the
separate, legacy `ledgerkit.models.Query` dataclass shape used
elsewhere in the project — through one evaluator, so every filtering
operation in ledgerkit is governed by the same semantics
(query-engine@v1#query-engine-015).

### AST node types

Nine frozen dataclasses, unioned into the `QueryNode` type alias, with
no shared base class or method — every consumer dispatches on them via
`isinstance` chains (query-engine@v1#query-engine-001):

- `Acct(pattern: str)`
- `Desc(pattern: str)`
- `DateSpan(start: Optional[date], end: Optional[date])` — `end` is
  exclusive
- `MaxAccountLevel(n: int)`
- `Status(value: TxnStatus)` — `TxnStatus` is an enum of `UNMARKED`,
  `PENDING`, `CLEARED`
- `Tag(name_pattern: str, value_pattern: Optional[str] = None)`
- `And(terms: tuple[QueryNode, ...])`
- `Or(terms: tuple[QueryNode, ...])`
- `Not(term: QueryNode)`

`MaxAccountLevel` is reachable only by constructing it directly in
Python — there is no string-query spelling that ever produces one; it
is deliberately distinct from hledger's own `depth:`/`--depth`, which
is a report-display option, not a selection predicate, and is modelled
separately (below) (query-engine@v1#query-engine-002).

### `QueryPlan(predicate: QueryNode, depth: DepthSpec = DepthSpec())`

The result of `parse()`: a predicate tree plus a separately-carried
`DepthSpec`. `predicate` never contains a `depth:` term — `parse()`
intercepts every `depth:`-prefixed token before the combination step
and merges it into the accumulated `DepthSpec` instead; a query
consisting solely of `depth:` terms produces `predicate == And(())`,
the vacuous "match everything" node
(query-engine@v1#query-engine-003).

### `parse(query_text: str) -> QueryPlan`

Tokenizes on whitespace while keeping a `prefix:"quoted phrase"`
together as one token with its quotes stripped; both single and
double quotes are accepted, escaping a quote character inside a
quoted token is not supported, and an unterminated quote degrades to
treating the remainder as plain literal text rather than raising
(query-engine@v1#query-engine-006).

After tokenizing, unnegated terms of the same node type (`Acct`,
`Desc`, or `Status`) are OR'd together into one bucket each; every
other term — `DateSpan`, `Tag`, and crucially *any* `Not`-wrapped term
regardless of its inner prefix — is AND'd in individually alongside
the OR buckets. A negated same-prefix term (e.g. two `not:acct:`
terms) is never folded into that prefix's OR bucket even when
unnegated terms of the same prefix are also present
(query-engine@v1#query-engine-005).

`date:` accepts exactly four value shapes: a closed range (two full
dates joined by `-`, `..`, or `" to "`), an open-from range (`D..`),
an open-to range (`..D`), or a single bare date. A single bare date `D`
becomes `DateSpan(D, D + 1 day)`; a written range's end date becomes
the literal, exclusive bound exactly as given, with no auto-increment
— so a range written as if it were inclusive is silently one day short
of the single-date case's own convention. A four-digit year is
mandatory; two-digit years are rejected (query-engine@v1#query-engine-007).

`depth:REGEX=N` and `tag:NAME=REGEX` both split their value on the
*first* `=` character only, so a later literal `=` is preserved
verbatim in the right-hand side (for example `tag:rate==0.05` becomes
a tag named `rate` with value `=0.05`). `not:depth:...`, with any
number of `not:` wraps, is rejected at parse time with
`QueryParseError`, since depth is a report option, not a negatable
predicate (query-engine@v1#query-engine-008).

### `matches_transaction(node, txn, journal=None) -> bool` / `matches_posting(node, txn, posting, journal=None) -> bool`

Evaluate a `QueryNode` against a transaction, or against one specific
posting of a transaction. They agree on every node type except how
`Acct`/`MaxAccountLevel` are checked: at the transaction level, they
match if *any* posting in the transaction satisfies the account-level
check; at the posting level, only that one posting's own account is
checked. `Desc`, `DateSpan`, and `Status` are transaction-level facts
and are evaluated identically by both functions. All text matching
(`Acct`/`Desc`) is case-insensitive, infix (substring) matching —
always `.search()`, never `.match()`/`.fullmatch()`
(query-engine@v1#query-engine-009).

A `Tag` node's evaluation is the one case where `journal` matters: both
functions raise `ValueError` if a `Tag` node is evaluated while
`journal` is `None` — this is a deliberate "loud failure" rather than
silently narrowing to a smaller tag set, and every other node type is
unaffected by `journal` being absent (query-engine@v1#query-engine-004).
When a `journal` is supplied, `Tag` matching uses the full four-source
"effective tags" of the (transaction, posting) pair described under
`ledgerkit.tags` above, with no shadowing between sources; at the
transaction level, a `Tag` node matches if the transaction's own tags
match *or* if any posting's effective tags match, checked as two
independent conditions (query-engine@v1#query-engine-010).

A second, package-private posting matcher sharing the same recursive
`And`/`Or`/`Not` dispatch body, parameterised by which tag-source
function it calls, backs a narrower accounts-mode tag visibility (the
union described under `ledgerkit.tags` above) — it is not part of the
public surface, but its existence explains why `ledgerkit.reports.accounts`'s
tag-query behaviour can differ from `matches_posting`'s own default
visibility for the same journal (query-engine@v1#query-engine-011).

### `DepthSpec`, `clipped_depth_for_account`, `clip_account_name`, `merge_depth_specs`

`DepthSpec` is never a selection filter — it is a report-display
clipping/aggregation option, deliberately kept outside the predicate
AST. It has two fields: `flat: Optional[int]`, a general clip depth,
and `by_pattern: tuple[tuple[str, int], ...]`, an ordered list of
`(regex, depth)` pairs contributed by `depth:REGEX=N` terms
(query-engine@v1#query-engine-014).

`merge_depth_specs` combines two specs by taking the **minimum** of
their `flat` values (treating `None` as "no constraint") and
concatenating `by_pattern` (query-engine@v1#query-engine-003,
query-engine@v1#query-engine-014).

`clipped_depth_for_account` resolves one account's effective clip
depth using a counterintuitive specificity rule: among the
`by_pattern` entries that match the account itself or any strict
ancestor, the entry whose matched ancestor is *deepest* — or that
matches the account itself but no strict ancestor at all, the single
most-specific case — wins; ties between equally-specific entries go to
the later-declared one. If no `by_pattern` entry matches, `flat` is the
fallback, and if that is also unset the account is not clipped.
`clip_account_name` renders a depth-0 clip as the literal string
`"..."`, never an empty string and never exclusion
(query-engine@v1#query-engine-014). No test in the permitted evidence
for this topic directly exercises `clipped_depth_for_account` or
`clip_account_name` in isolation — this specificity and tie-break rule
was confirmed by direct execution rather than by an existing unit
test, a gap independently rediscovered by a model-blind implementation
reconstruction of the same module
(query-engine@v1#query-engine-014).

A third, separate function in the same module,
`account_excluded_by_depth`, is a genuine boolean-exclusion check
(stated to be used only by `ledgerkit.reports.stats`) rather than a
clipping function — conflating it with `clipped_depth_for_account`
would be a real correctness bug for any caller extending
depth-aware reporting (query-engine@v1#query-engine-014).

### `validate_hledger_regex(pattern: str) -> None` / `compile_hledger_regex(pattern: str) -> re.Pattern`

Implement a restricted "HledgerRegex" dialect on top of Python's `re`,
deliberately never silently reinterpreting a construct using Python's
own semantics where it would differ from hledger's regex engine
(query-engine@v1#query-engine-012). A pattern is rejected, raising
`UnsupportedRegexConstructError` (a `ValueError` subclass), if it
contains: an inline-flag/named/non-capturing/lookaround `(?...)`
construct; a numeric backreference (`\1`–`\9`); a GNU word-boundary
escape (`\<`/`\>`); a Perl shorthand class (`\d`/`\D`/`\w`/`\W`/`\s`/`\S`);
a POSIX named class (`[[:alpha:]]`-style); or a lazy/non-greedy
quantifier (`*?`/`+?`/`??`). A pattern that is empty, or contains an
unescaped `|` with nothing on one side of it (detected by a dedicated
escape-aware scan rather than a single compiled regex, since
distinguishing a real `|` from an escaped `\|` needs variable-length
backslash-parity counting that a fixed-width `re` lookbehind cannot
express), is also rejected — both of these two additional rejections
are documented as breaking changes from this module's own earlier
behaviour. A pattern that merely admits an empty match (such as `.*`
or `a*`) remains valid; only the literal empty string itself is
rejected (query-engine@v1#query-engine-012,
query-engine@v1#query-engine-013). `compile_hledger_regex` always
compiles with `re.IGNORECASE`.

