## `ledgerkit.reports`

Pure report-computation functions; all CLI-facing formatting (amount
display, column alignment, ANSI colouring) lives in `ledgerkit.cli`,
not here (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-005, reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-007).

### `balance(journal, query=None, tree=False, ...) -> dict[str, dict[str, Decimal]] | list[BalanceRow]`

By default returns a flat mapping of account name to a mapping of
commodity to net `Decimal`. With `tree=True`, instead returns
`list[BalanceRow]`, additionally including every implicit parent
account (every colon-prefix of every posted-to account) with
`is_subtotal=True` for pure-parent rows, each row's amounts aggregated
over itself plus all descendants, sorted alphabetically by account
name (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-004).

### `register(journal, query=None, ...) -> list[RegisterRow]`

Returns a chronological list of rows, one per posting. Per the
assertion's own description, `running_balance` is the cumulative sum
of `amount.quantity` across *all* rows in output order — never
re-grouped or re-aggregated by account, even when a depth setting
clips several postings' displayed account names to the same string
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-006). This specific invariant was not
independently re-confirmed by a model-blind implementation
reconstruction of this module (the reconstruction's own evidence did
not extend to exercising depth-clipping together with
`running_balance`), so treat it as asserted and internally
well-evidenced, but not doubly corroborated
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-006).

### `accounts(journal, query=None, ...) -> list[str]`

Returns the sorted, deduplicated list of account names matching the
given query (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-008).

### `stats(journal, query=None, ...) -> JournalStats`

Returns a dataclass containing only deterministic journal data
(transaction count, date range, per-day rates, payee/account/commodity
counts, and so on); runtime figures such as elapsed time and
transactions-per-second are measured and formatted only by the CLI
layer, not stored on `JournalStats` (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-011).
`stats` is the one report function in this module where a `depth:`
constraint **excludes** accounts deeper than the limit from
`account_count`/`account_depth`, via a genuinely boolean-exclusion
function, rather than clipping and aggregating them the way
`balance`/`register`/`accounts` all do — documented as a real,
source-confirmed hledger quirk rather than a ledgerkit invention
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-012).

### `balance_from_spec(journal, spec, query=None) -> list[ReportSectionResult]`

Computes a structured, multi-section balance report (for example an
income statement, with sections such as "Income" marked `invert=True`
and "Expenses" not) driven by `ReportSpec`/`ReportSection` dataclasses,
returning one `ReportSectionResult` per section. **This function is
Python-API-only** — it is reachable from `ledgerkit.balance_from_spec`,
`ledgerkit.ReportSpec`, and `ledgerkit.ReportSection`, but none of the
six CLI subcommands (`balance`, `register`, `accounts`, `print`,
`stats`, `check`) ever invoke it; there is no CLI command that wires
to it (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-013).

`ReportSectionResult.to_dataframe()` carries a known, self-acknowledged
single-commodity limitation: its own docstring states multi-commodity
support is deferred, and its implementation picks one commodity —
taken from an arbitrary/first key of the journal's commodity-styles
map, not a per-row lookup — and applies that one style to every row
regardless of which commodity each row's value actually originated in
(commodity-styling@v1#commodity-styling-008).

