## `ledgerkit.checks`

### Tier constants

Three fixed tuples, mirroring hledger's own classification
(checks@v1#checks-001):

- `BASIC_CHECK_NAMES = ("parseable", "autobalanced", "assertions")` —
  run by default on every CLI command.
- `STRICT_CHECK_NAMES = ("accounts", "commodities")` — run only when
  strict mode is requested, in addition to the basic tier.
- `OTHER_CHECK_NAMES = ("payees", "ordereddates", "uniqueleafnames")` —
  run only when explicitly named.

`ALL_CHECK_NAMES` is their concatenation.

### Individual check functions

Each has the signature `(journal: Journal) -> list[CheckError]`
(`CheckError` has `check_name: str`, `message: str`, `line_number:
Optional[int] = None`). Checks never print — formatting and display is
entirely the caller's responsibility (checks@v1#checks-001).

- **`check_parseable`** — a no-op: by the time a `Journal` object
  exists, parseability has already been established by the parser, so
  this always returns `[]`.
- **`check_autobalanced`** / **`check_transaction_autobalanced`** —
  treat a transaction as balanced if it has exactly one elided posting
  (`amount is None`), regardless of how many commodities the other
  postings use; two or more elided postings unconditionally fails with
  a "multiple elided postings" error; with zero elided postings, every
  commodity's net sum across the transaction's postings must equal
  exactly zero, with one `CheckError` per offending commodity
  (checks@v1#checks-002).
- **`check_accounts`** — reports at most one `CheckError` per run,
  stopping at the very first posting (in transaction, then posting,
  order) whose account is not in `journal.declared_accounts`
  (case-sensitive); if `declared_accounts` is empty, every account is
  considered undeclared, so the first posting's account is the sole
  error (checks@v1#checks-003).
- **`check_commodities`** — the same stop-at-first behaviour for the
  first posting whose non-empty commodity symbol is not in
  `journal.declared_commodities`; a posting with no amount is skipped
  entirely, and a posting whose amount's commodity symbol is the
  *empty string* (not merely a zero quantity) is always exempt from
  this check (checks@v1#checks-004).
- **`check_payees`** — flags each distinct transaction `description`
  not present in `declared_payees` exactly once, regardless of how many
  transactions repeat that description; flags everything if
  `declared_payees` is empty or unset (checks@v1#checks-007).
- **`check_ordereddates`** — flags a transaction whose date is less
  than a running high-water mark, not the literal immediately-preceding
  transaction's date: the mark advances to a transaction's own date
  only when that transaction itself was *not* flagged, so a flagged,
  out-of-order transaction leaves the comparison baseline unchanged for
  whatever comes after it (checks@v1#checks-007). Same-date
  transactions never trigger it.
- **`check_uniqueleafnames`** — flags every account-name "leaf" (the
  text after the last `:`, or the whole name if there is no `:`) that
  is shared by two or more distinct full account names anywhere in the
  journal (checks@v1#checks-007).
- **`check_assertions`** — validates all four balance-assertion
  variants (`=`, `==`, `=*`, `==*`, modelled as the `inclusive` and
  `sole_commodity` flags on `BalanceAssertion`), processing postings in
  `(date, source_line or 0)` order — **not** journal/parse order — and
  maintaining a running per-account-per-commodity balance. A mismatch
  between the expected and actual balance, and (independently) a
  sole-commodity violation, each append their own separate
  `CheckError`, so a single posting can produce more than one error
  (checks@v1#checks-006).

Strict-tier failures (`accounts`, `commodities`) produce a distinctive
multi-line `CheckError.message`: a file:line header, a reconstructed
transaction/posting source line, a caret underline positioned under
the offending account name or amount text, an explanation, and example
`account`/`commodity` directive suggestions — every other check's
message is a single-line plain string
(checks@v1#checks-005).

### Runners

- **`run_basic_checks(journal, *, skip=None) -> list[CheckError]`** —
  runs every name in `BASIC_CHECK_NAMES` except any present in `skip`.
- **`run_strict_checks(journal, *, skip=None) -> list[CheckError]`** —
  runs the basic tier (with `skip` applied there) and then
  unconditionally appends every `STRICT_CHECK_NAMES` result; `skip` is
  **not** applied to the strict-tier names themselves.
- **`run_checks(journal, names=None, *, strict=False, skip=None) -> list[CheckError]`** —
  validates `names` against the internal check registry up front,
  raising `ValueError` if any name is unrecognised; always runs the
  basic tier (minus `skip`); additionally runs the strict tier,
  unconditionally with respect to `skip`, if `strict=True`; then runs
  every remaining name in `names` that is not already a member of a
  prior tier's name set (checks@v1#checks-001,
  checks@v1#checks-008).

  This "already run" membership test is a **static union of tier name
  sets**, not a record of what actually executed. Two confirmed
  consequences follow, and whether either is intentional design or an
  oversight is not resolved as of this writing:

  1. A basic-tier (or, under `strict=True`, strict-tier) name that is
     both suppressed via `skip` and explicitly re-requested via
     `names` executes **zero** times, not once — `skip` suppresses the
     tier's own run, and the static membership check then also blocks
     the explicit `names`-loop from giving it a second chance
     (checks@v1#checks-008).
  2. `skip` is never consulted by `run_checks`'s own strict-tier
     branch, so a name present in both `skip` and `STRICT_CHECK_NAMES`
     still runs whenever `strict=True` — the opposite failure mode
     from (1) (checks@v1#checks-008).

  An independent, model-blind implementation reconstruction of this
  module corroborated the coarse-grained dedup/validation behaviour of
  `run_checks`, and separately found the identical kind of asymmetry
  in `run_checks`'s sibling function `run_strict_checks` (whose own
  `skip` parameter only ever reaches the basic-tier call inside it),
  but did not independently re-test the two sharper claims above for
  `run_checks` itself — treat them as asserted from direct code
  reading and reproduction, not doubly corroborated
  (checks@v1#checks-008).

