## `ledgerkit.editor_model`

### `EditorDocument`

A single public class intended for in-memory, span-tracked editing of
one journal file by a TUI/editor frontend. Its own module docstring
states it is "Intended for use by TUI/editor frontends. Not imported
by any core module" (editor-surface@v1#editor-surface-005).

Public attributes: `path: str`, `journal: Journal`, `lines: list[str]`,
`dirty: bool`. Public methods: `__init__(self, path: str)`,
`add_transaction(self, txn: Transaction) -> None`,
`update_transaction(self, original: Transaction, updated: Transaction)
-> None`, `delete_transaction(self, txn: Transaction) -> None`,
`save(self) -> None`, `reload(self) -> None`,
`validate_transaction(self, txn: Transaction) -> list[CheckError]`
(editor-surface@v1#editor-surface-001).

`__init__` loads the journal via `ledgerkit.parser.parse_string()`
directly — **not** `ledgerkit.loader.load_journal()` — and so does not
process `include` directives at all. This is a stated v1 limitation
with an explicit pointer in the class's own docstring to use
`loader.load_journal()` when full include support is required
(editor-surface@v1#editor-surface-002).

Each of the three mutation methods (`add_transaction`,
`update_transaction`, `delete_transaction`) sets `self.dirty = True`
after mutating `self.lines` and `self.journal.transactions`, and
recomputes `source_span` for affected/subsequent transactions so that
line-number bookkeeping stays consistent with the in-memory text
buffer; the span shift is conditional on a non-zero line-count delta,
not unconditional (for example, replacing a transaction with one that
serialises to the same number of lines does not shift later spans)
(editor-surface@v1#editor-surface-003). `update_transaction` and
`delete_transaction` both require the given transaction to already
carry a `source_span` (editor-surface@v1#editor-surface-003).

`validate_transaction(txn)` runs only the single "autobalanced" check
(`ledgerkit.checks.check_transaction_autobalanced`) against the given
transaction in isolation, returns a possibly-empty list of
`CheckError`, and never raises
(editor-surface@v1#editor-surface-004).

### A note on current adoption

`EditorDocument` is implemented and unit-tested inside ledgerkit
itself, and its own docstring names "TUI/editor frontends" as its
intended consumer. As of the pinned external revision examined for
this clean-room effort, the one real such frontend project
(`ledgerkit-editor`) that depends on `ledgerkit` **does not import or
instantiate `EditorDocument` anywhere** in its source or test code —
an exhaustive search of that project found the class named only in two
non-executable places, a docstring line and a `# TODO` comment
describing a not-yet-implemented future action
(editor-surface@v1#editor-surface-007). That project's six other
runtime import sites into `ledgerkit` instead call
`parse_string_lenient`, `load`, `transaction_to_text`,
`journal_to_text`, and construct `Query` directly — the same
lower-level functions and types documented elsewhere in this document
(editor-surface@v1#editor-surface-008,
editor-surface@v1#editor-surface-009). This is a fact about current
external adoption, not a reason to omit `EditorDocument`'s own
documented behaviour above, which remains fully functional and tested
within ledgerkit's own suite.

